## Why it fails

`&v[0]` creates an immutable borrow of `v` that lasts until `first` is last used. `v.push(4)` requires a mutable borrow of `v` (which may reallocate the vector's heap buffer). The borrow checker rejects this because a mutable operation on `v` could invalidate the reference `first` points to — if `push` reallocates, `first` would be a dangling pointer.
```error[E0502]: cannot borrow `v` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable
 --> src/main.rs:3:6
  |
2 |     let first = &v[0];
  |                   ----- immutable borrow occurs
3 |     v.push(4);
  |     ^ mutable borrow occurs here
...
5 |     println!("{}", first);
  |                       ----- immutable borrow used here
```
## Fix 1: Copy the value out before mutating
```rustfn main() {
    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let first = v[0];       // Copy (i32 implements Copy)
    v.push(4);
    println!("{}", first);
}
```
`v[0]` returns `i32` by value (not a reference), so no borrow exists when `push` runs.

## Fix 2: Limit the borrow's scope so it ends before `push`
```rustfn main() {
    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let first = {
        let r = &v[0];
        *r
    };                       // borrow ends here
    v.push(4);
    println!("{}", first);
}
```
The immutable borrow is confined to a block and ends before `push`, so the mutable borrow is allowed. The value is dereferenced out of the reference before the borrow expires.
